[size=5][b]POI Map Plus[/b][/size]

[size=4][b]Description[/b][/size]

You clear a POI, drive on, and three days later you are standing in front of the same building wondering whether you have already stripped it. POI Map Plus answers that from the map screen.

Every POI you have been to gets a skull marker. Orange means the good loot is still in there. Green means you already took it. The marker also shows the difficulty tier, and when the game respawn loot over time, it goes orange again on its own.

It is a quality of life mod: no new blocks, no new items, no balance changes. It only reads what the game already knows and puts it on the map.

[size=4][b]What the marker tells you[/b][/size]

[list]
[*][b]The skull[/b] - orange while the best container in that POI is still untouched, green once it has been opened.
[*][b]The tier[/b] - printed right next to the skull, so you can plan a run without hovering over anything. White for the easy ones, orange for tier 3, red for tier 4 and up.
[*][b]The chest badge[/b] - green if you emptied the container, orange if you left something in it. That matters because the game only refills containers that were left completely empty.
[/list]

Point at a marker and the tooltip spells it out in words as well.

[size=4][b]When the loot comes back[/b][/size]

If loot respawn is on in your world, a cleared POI is worth robbing again later. When that day comes the marker goes orange again by itself, and the POI looks exactly like one you have never touched (the skull itself stays on the map, of course - you have already been there and it remains discovered), so you can plan from the map without remembering what you cleared and when.

One thing worth knowing, and it comes from the game rather than the mod: a container is only ever refilled if you left it completely empty. Leave a single piece of junk in there and it will never be restocked - what you left stays where it is, but no new loot will ever appear in it. The green chest badge is how you tell the difference.

[size=4][b]Settings on the map[/b][/size]

There is a small gear in the bottom right corner of the map. It opens a panel where you can:

[list]
[*]turn the markers off entirely, without losing anything - the mod keeps tracking in the background and everything is still there when you switch them back on;
[*]hide the tier label or the chest badge if you prefer a cleaner map;
[*]pick the marker size: small, medium or large.
[/list]

Your choice is remembered per save.

[size=4][b]Main features[/b][/size]

[list]
[*][b]Markers for POIs you have visited.[/b] A marker appears the first time you get close enough for that part of the map to uncover, and stays from then on. Nothing is revealed about places you have never approached.
[*][b]Tier and loot state at a glance.[/b] No hovering needed for the basics.
[*][b]The map keeps up on its own.[/b] When the game puts loot back into a POI you cleared, its marker goes orange again without you having to go and look.
[*][b]Follows the best container present.[/b] The mod ranks loot containers and watches the richest one a POI actually has, so a tier 5 chest is what counts in a big dungeon, and a weapons bag is what counts where that is all there is.
[*][b]Quest resets are respected.[/b] Reset a POI through a trader quest and its marker goes back to orange.
[*][b]Works on existing saves.[/b]
[/list]

[size=4][b]How "cleared" is decided[/b][/size]

The mod follows a ranked list of loot containers, from the richest down. In each POI it picks the best ranked container that is actually there and watches that one; lower ranks only matter when nothing better exists in that POI. An entry can be limited to small POIs - a weapons bag is the prize of a tier 1 house, but in a tier 5 dungeon it means nothing, so by default it only counts in tier 1.

Trash bins, mailboxes and other filler are not on the list at all, so opening a nightstand on the way in does not mark anything. A POI holding nothing from the list is marked cleared straight away. The full list sits in PoiMapPlus.xml and can be reordered or extended.

[size=4][b]Requirements[/b][/size]

[list]
[*]7 Days to Die [b]V 3.3[/b]. On older game versions use file 3.3 from the Files tab.
[*]The game must run [b]without EasyAntiCheat[/b]. The mod ships a DLL and EAC blocks those.
[/list]

[size=4][b]Installation instructions[/b][/size]

[list=1]
[*]Download the archive (or install via Vortex).
[*]Extract it into your Mods folder so that you end up with [b]Mods\PoiMapPlus\ModInfo.xml[/b]. If your game has no Mods folder yet, create one next to 7DaysToDie.exe.
[*]Start the game without EasyAntiCheat.
[/list]

Vortex and the 7D2D Mod Launcher handle the archive as a normal modlet. To uninstall, delete the folder - the mod only ever writes its own small state file into your save.

[size=4][b]Multiplayer[/b][/size]

The mod is client side only: the server does not need it and neither do the other players. Everything it reads is already on your client, and looted marks live in your own save.

[b]That said, it has only been tested in single player.[/b] It should behave the same as a client on a dedicated server, but nobody has verified that yet - feedback welcome. Marks are personal: they do not sync with what your teammates have looted.

[size=4][b]Compatibility[/b][/size]

Hooks vanilla methods with Harmony postfixes and appends a single nav object class through XPath, so no vanilla config file is overwritten. Both marker icons come from the game's own icon set, so a UI overhaul that restyles the interface restyles them too. UI mods that only change how the map looks are fine; a mod that replaces the map controller with its own class would leave the markers unhooked. Containers added by other mods are supported - put their loot lists into the config. Large overhauls such as Darkness Falls or Undead Legacy rework loot completely, so the default ranking needs adjusting for them.

[size=4][b]Configuration[/b][/size]

Most of it is on the map itself, behind the gear. For the rest there is [b]PoiMapPlus.xml[/b] next to ModInfo.xml, with comments explaining each setting. The ones people usually touch:

[list]
[*][b]MinTier[/b] - lowest POI tier that gets a marker. Default 1, which skips tier 0 sheds and ruined booths. Set it to 0 to see everything.
[*][b]OnlyDiscovered[/b] - set to false to reveal every POI in the world from the start.
[*][b]tracked_loot_lists[/b] - which containers count and in what order.
[*]Marker looks - size, badge placement and the colour bands for the tier label.
[/list]

[size=4][b]Changelog[/b][/size]

[b]3.3.1[/b]
[list]
[*]Works with game version 3.3.
[*]Removed the Russian translation.
[/list]

[b]3.3[/b]
[list]
[*]The marker now shows the POI tier, so you no longer have to hover to see how rough a place is.
[*]Added a chest badge: green if you emptied the container, orange if you left something in it. Only emptied ones ever get refilled by the game.
[*]When the game puts loot back into a cleared POI, its marker goes orange again by itself.
[*]Added a settings panel on the map, behind the gear in the bottom corner: markers on and off, tier label, chest badge, and three marker sizes. Remembered per save.
[*]The tooltip now says what happened to the place, not just its name.
[*]Fixed the map occasionally losing all of its icons for a moment.
[/list]

[b]3.2[/b]
[list]
[*]First release.
[/list]

[size=4][b]Source and permissions[/b][/size]

Source code is on GitHub: [url=https://github.com/lyova/7dtd-mods]github.com/lyova/7dtd-mods[/url]

MIT licensed. Fork it, modify it, bundle it into mod packs - credit is appreciated but not required.

